CAT = "pknleMedicine"; SEG = "MED"
Q = [
('A 58-year-old man has crushing chest pain and ST elevation in leads V1 to V4. Which artery is most likely blocked?',
 'Left anterior descending', ['Right coronary artery', 'Left circumflex artery', 'Posterior descending'],
 'ST elevation in the anterior chest leads V1 to V4 points to an anterior infarct from the left anterior descending artery. Inferior leads II, III and aVF point to the right coronary artery.'),
("A patient with STEMI reaches a hospital with no catheter lab and transfer would take three hours. What is the next step if there is no contraindication?",
 "Give a thrombolytic", ["Wait for transfer", "Start heparin only", "Give oral nitrates only"],
 "When primary PCI cannot be done within about 120 minutes of diagnosis, fibrinolysis is given, ideally within 10 minutes of diagnosis, followed by transfer for angiography."),
("A woman with irregularly irregular pulse at 140 per minute is stable. ECG shows no P waves. What is the rhythm?",
 "Atrial fibrillation", ["Atrial flutter", "Sinus tachycardia", "Ventricular tachycardia"],
 "Absent P waves with an irregularly irregular ventricular rhythm is atrial fibrillation. Flutter shows saw-tooth waves and is often regular at about 150 per minute."),
("A 12-year-old has fever, a migrating polyarthritis and a new murmur three weeks after a sore throat. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
 "Acute rheumatic fever", ["Septic arthritis", "Juvenile idiopathic arthritis", "Reactive arthritis"],
 "Carditis and migratory polyarthritis after group A streptococcal pharyngitis are two major Jones criteria. Reactive arthritis follows gut or genital infection and spares the heart."),
("How is secondary prophylaxis usually given after rheumatic fever with carditis?",
 "IM benzathine penicillin", ["Oral amoxicillin, 10 days", "Six-monthly ceftriaxone", "Azithromycin when febrile"],
 "Monthly or three-weekly intramuscular benzathine penicillin G prevents recurrent streptococcal infection, which would further damage the valves. With carditis it continues for many years."),
("A drug user has fever and a new murmur. What should be done before starting antibiotics?",
 "Three blood cultures", ["A single urine culture", "A chest X-ray first", "A throat swab culture"],
 "Infective endocarditis is diagnosed largely on positive blood cultures, so three sets from separate sites are taken before antibiotics. Echocardiography follows."),
("A patient with heart failure on digoxin reports nausea and yellow-tinted vision. What is the most likely cause?",
 "Digoxin toxicity", ["Acute cataract", "Acute hepatitis", "Hyponatraemia"],
 "Xanthopsia, nausea and arrhythmias are classic features of digoxin toxicity, which hypokalaemia and kidney impairment make more likely. Levels are checked and the drug stopped."),
("A patient has a blood pressure of 230/140 mmHg with papilloedema and a rising creatinine. How fast should the mean arterial pressure be lowered in the first hour?",
 "By no more than 25%", ["To normal within minutes", "By 50% at once", "Not at all for 24 hours"],
 "In a hypertensive emergency, a controlled fall of about a quarter in the first hour avoids cerebral and renal ischaemia from an abrupt drop. Further lowering follows over 24 to 48 hours."),
("An asthmatic woman cannot complete sentences and her peak flow is 40% of her best. How is this attack classed?",
 "Acute severe asthma", ["Moderate asthma", "Life-threatening asthma", "Near-fatal asthma"],
 "Peak flow of 33 to 50% of best or predicted, or inability to complete sentences, marks acute severe asthma. Below 33%, silent chest or exhaustion marks it as life-threatening."),
("Which drug is the first treatment for an acute asthma attack in the emergency room?",
 "Nebulised salbutamol", ["Oral montelukast", "Inhaled salmeterol", "Oral theophylline"],
 "A short-acting beta-2 agonist, nebulised with oxygen, relieves bronchospasm within minutes. Salmeterol is long-acting and is not a rescue drug."),
("A COPD patient is on oxygen and becomes drowsy with a rising carbon dioxide. What target oxygen saturation is usually advised?",
 "88 to 92%", ["94 to 98%", "98 to 100%", "80 to 84%"],
 "Patients at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure are given controlled oxygen to keep saturation at 88 to 92%. Higher targets can worsen carbon dioxide retention."),
("A woman two weeks after a caesarean section has sudden breathlessness, pleuritic pain and tachycardia. Which scan best confirms the diagnosis?",
 "CT pulmonary angiogram", ["Plain chest X-ray", "Abdominal ultrasound", "Echocardiogram alone"],
 "A pulmonary embolism is likely in the postpartum period. CTPA is the usual confirmatory test; a chest X-ray is often normal and only rules out other causes."),
("A 70-year-old has pneumonia, is confused and has a respiratory rate of 32. What is his CURB-65 score from these findings alone?",
 "3", ["2", "1", "4"],
 "He scores one each for confusion, respiratory rate of 30 or more and age 65 or over, so 3. Urea and blood pressure would add points if abnormal; 3 or more is severe pneumonia."),
("A man has had a cough for three weeks, night sweats and weight loss. Which rapid first test, recommended by WHO, also detects rifampicin resistance?",
 "GeneXpert MTB/RIF", ["Mantoux test", "Chest X-ray alone", "Serum TB antibody"],
 "WHO recommends rapid molecular tests such as Xpert MTB/RIF as the initial diagnostic test; it detects TB DNA in sputum and rifampicin resistance within hours. Serological TB tests are not recommended."),
("A patient from Lahore has hepatitis C with detectable HCV RNA and no cirrhosis. Which pangenotypic regimen is the most widely used and lowest cost?",
 "Sofosbuvir with daclatasvir", ["Pegylated interferon alone", "Tenofovir with lamivudine", "Ribavirin alone"],
 "WHO calls sofosbuvir with daclatasvir the most widely used low-cost pangenotypic direct-acting antiviral regimen; a 12 to 24 week course cures over 95%. Tenofovir and lamivudine treat hepatitis B, not C."),
("Which drug is a first-line oral treatment for chronic hepatitis B?",
 "Tenofovir", ["Sofosbuvir", "Acyclovir", "Oseltamivir"],
 "Tenofovir and entecavir have a high barrier to resistance and suppress HBV DNA. Sofosbuvir works against hepatitis C only."),
("A man with cirrhosis and ascites has fever and abdominal pain. Ascitic fluid shows 400 neutrophils per mm3. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis", ["Tuberculous peritonitis", "Hepatorenal syndrome", "Portal vein thrombosis"],
 "An ascitic neutrophil count above 250 per mm3 diagnoses SBP. It is treated with an IV third-generation cephalosporin and albumin."),
("A cirrhotic patient vomits large amounts of fresh blood. Along with resuscitation, which drug is started before endoscopy?",
 "Terlipressin", ["Propranolol", "Omeprazole infusion only", "Tranexamic acid"],
 "Suspected variceal bleeding is treated with a vasoactive drug such as terlipressin or octreotide and prophylactic antibiotics, then band ligation. Beta-blockers are for prevention, not active bleeding."),
('A man with cirrhosis grows drowsy and disorientated two days after a variceal bleed. Which drug lowers his gut ammonia?',
 'Lactulose', ['Diazepam', 'Morphine', 'Furosemide'],
 'Blood in the gut is a protein load that raises ammonia and triggers hepatic encephalopathy. Lactulose acidifies the colon and speeds transit, reducing ammonia absorption; sedatives worsen it.'),
("A young woman has bloody diarrhoea and colonoscopy shows continuous inflammation from the rectum upwards. What is the likely diagnosis?",
 "Ulcerative colitis", ["Crohn's disease", "Amoebic colitis", "Irritable bowel syndrome"],
 "Ulcerative colitis starts in the rectum and spreads proximally without skip lesions, limited to the mucosa. Crohn's disease has skip lesions and transmural inflammation."),
("Which blood test is best for screening a patient on a normal diet for coeliac disease?",
 "IgA tissue transglutaminase", ["Anti-mitochondrial antibody", "Anti-smooth muscle antibody", "Serum amylase"],
 "IgA anti-tTG with total IgA is the first-line test, and duodenal biopsy confirms it. Anti-mitochondrial antibodies point to primary biliary cholangitis."),
("A 19-year-old with type 1 diabetes has glucose 28 mmol/L, ketones and pH 7.1. What is the first step in management?",
 "IV 0.9% saline", ["IV insulin bolus first", "IV sodium bicarbonate", "Oral rehydration"],
 "In DKA, fluid replacement with 0.9% saline comes first, then a fixed-rate insulin infusion about 0.1 units/kg/hour, with potassium added as needed. Bicarbonate is not routine."),
("A woman with weight loss, tremor, eye protrusion and a diffuse goitre most likely has which condition?",
 "Graves' disease", ["Hashimoto's thyroiditis", "Toxic adenoma", "De Quervain's thyroiditis"],
 "Exophthalmos and a diffuse goitre with hyperthyroidism point to Graves' disease, caused by TSH receptor stimulating antibodies. A toxic adenoma gives a single nodule."),
("A patient on long-term steroids stops them suddenly and presents in shock with low sodium and high potassium. What is given immediately?",
 "IV hydrocortisone", ["IV fludrocortisone", "Oral prednisolone", "IV insulin"],
 "This is an adrenal crisis. Hydrocortisone 100 mg IV with fluids is given at once; at that dose it also provides mineralocorticoid effect."),
('A patient with a potassium of 7.1 mmol/L has had calcium gluconate. Which treatment then moves potassium into cells fastest?',
 'Insulin with dextrose', ['Calcium resonium', 'IV furosemide', 'Oral sodium bicarbonate'],
 'Insulin drives potassium into cells within 15 to 30 minutes, with dextrose to prevent hypoglycaemia. Calcium resonium removes potassium from the body but takes hours.'),
("A patient has chronic hyponatraemia with a sodium of 112 mmol/L. Why should correction be limited to about 8 to 10 mmol/L in 24 hours?",
 "To avoid demyelination", ["To avoid hyperkalaemia", "To avoid fluid overload", "To avoid renal stones"],
 "Rapid correction of chronic hyponatraemia can cause osmotic demyelination (central pontine myelinolysis), with dysarthria, quadriparesis and locked-in state."),
("A child has puffy eyes, heavy proteinuria and a low albumin with normal blood pressure. What is the most likely cause?",
 "Minimal change disease", ["Membranous nephropathy", "IgA nephropathy", "Post-infectious nephritis"],
 "Minimal change disease is the commonest cause of nephrotic syndrome in children and responds well to steroids. Membranous nephropathy is commoner in adults."),
("A 65-year-old has sudden right arm weakness and slurred speech that began 2 hours ago. CT shows no bleed. What is the standard treatment if eligible?",
 "IV thrombolysis", ["Aspirin and wait 48 hours", "IV mannitol", "Immediate warfarin"],
 "Alteplase can be given within 4.5 hours of onset of an ischaemic stroke once bleeding is excluded. Large vessel occlusions may also need thrombectomy."),
("A student has fever, neck stiffness and a purpuric rash. Which antibiotic is given without delay?",
 "IV ceftriaxone", ["Oral amoxicillin", "IV gentamicin alone", "Oral ciprofloxacin"],
 "Suspected meningococcal meningitis needs an IV third-generation cephalosporin at once, even before a lumbar puncture if that would delay treatment."),
("A man is still fitting after 5 minutes of a tonic-clonic seizure. What is the first drug if IV access is present?",
 "IV lorazepam", ["IV phenytoin", "IV carbamazepine", "Oral valproate"],
 "A benzodiazepine is the first-line treatment for status epilepticus. Phenytoin, levetiracetam or valproate follow if seizures continue."),
("Two weeks after a bout of diarrhoea, a man develops weakness spreading up from his legs with lost reflexes. What must be monitored closely?",
 "Vital capacity", ["Serum potassium", "Blood glucose", "Liver enzymes"],
 "This is Guillain-Barre syndrome, often after Campylobacter infection. Respiratory muscle weakness can follow quickly, so FVC is checked and ventilation arranged if it falls."),
("A woman has drooping eyelids and double vision that get worse by evening. Which test supports the diagnosis?",
 "Anti-AChR antibody", ["Anti-GAD antibody", "Anti-dsDNA antibody", "Serum CK level"],
 "Fatigable ptosis and diplopia suggest myasthenia gravis, and most generalised cases have antibodies to the acetylcholine receptor. CK is normal in myasthenia."),
("A man in Karachi has fever for 10 days. Blood culture grows Salmonella Typhi resistant to ceftriaxone and ciprofloxacin. Which oral drug is used for uncomplicated illness?",
 "Azithromycin", ["Chloramphenicol", "Co-trimoxazole", "Amoxicillin"],
 "Extensively drug-resistant typhoid, which began in Hyderabad, Sindh, in November 2016, resists first-line drugs, fluoroquinolones and third-generation cephalosporins. Azithromycin is used orally and a carbapenem for complicated cases."),
("A patient with dengue fever, day 5, now has abdominal pain, persistent vomiting and a rising haematocrit with falling platelets. What does this indicate?",
 "Dengue warning signs", ["Normal recovery phase", "Typhoid coinfection", "The usual febrile phase"],
 "Warning signs often appear as the fever settles, in the critical phase, when plasma leakage begins. The patient is admitted for careful IV fluids."),
("Which painkiller should be used for fever in suspected dengue?",
 "Paracetamol", ["Ibuprofen", "Aspirin", "Diclofenac"],
 "NSAIDs and aspirin increase the risk of bleeding and are avoided in dengue. Paracetamol in normal doses is used for fever and aches."),
("A man from Khyber Pakhtunkhwa has Plasmodium vivax malaria and a normal G6PD level. What is the standard treatment?",
 "Chloroquine then primaquine", ["Quinine with doxycycline", "Primaquine for 3 days only", "Chloroquine for 3 days only"],
 "Where P. vivax is still chloroquine sensitive, chloroquine clears the blood stage and primaquine, usually a 14-day course, kills the dormant liver hypnozoites to prevent relapse, after G6PD deficiency is excluded."),
("A patient with falciparum malaria is confused and jaundiced. What is the first-line treatment?",
 "IV artesunate", ["Oral chloroquine", "Oral primaquine", "IM penicillin"],
 "Severe falciparum malaria is treated with IV or IM artesunate for at least 24 hours, followed by a full course of an oral artemisinin combination."),
("A butcher has high fever, bleeding gums and low platelets a week after Eid ul Adha. Which infection is suspected?",
 "Congo fever (CCHF)", ["Acute brucellosis", "Weil's disease", "Acute hepatitis E"],
 "CCHF spreads by tick bites and by contact with blood of infected livestock at slaughter. It causes bleeding and low platelets and needs strict infection control; ribavirin has been given off label, though its benefit is unproven."),
("A boy was bitten on the face by a stray dog and the skin is broken with bleeding. Besides wound washing, what is needed?",
 "Vaccine plus RIG", ["Vaccine alone", "Tetanus toxoid alone", "Observe the dog"],
 "A bleeding bite is a category III exposure, so rabies immunoglobulin (RIG) is infiltrated into the wound and a full vaccine course is started. Face bites are high risk."),
("A shepherd has undulant fever, sweats and back pain after drinking raw milk. Which treatment is standard?",
 "Doxycycline with rifampicin", ["Penicillin and gentamicin", "Metronidazole for a week", "Ciprofloxacin for 5 days"],
 "Brucellosis is treated with a combination, because a single drug relapses: WHO lists doxycycline for 45 days with streptomycin, or with rifampicin for 45 days."),
("A child from Balochistan has a painless crusted ulcer on the cheek that began as a papule after insect bites. What is the likely cause?",
 "Cutaneous leishmaniasis", ["Cutaneous tuberculosis", "Basal cell carcinoma", "Impetigo"],
 "Female phlebotomine sandflies transmit Leishmania. The papule slowly ulcerates over weeks on exposed skin such as the face, and the ulcer can leave a lifelong scar."),
("A farmer is bitten by a snake. Blood in a dry glass tube fails to clot after 20 minutes. What does this show?",
 "Venom coagulopathy", ["Normal clotting", "Neurotoxic bite only", "Iron deficiency"],
 "The 20-minute whole blood clotting test flags coagulopathy from viper venom, an indication for polyvalent anti-snake venom."),
("A labourer collapses in June heat with a core temperature of 41.5 degrees C and confusion. What matters most?",
 "Rapid cooling", ["Oral paracetamol", "IV antibiotics", "Warm fluids"],
 "Heatstroke is a medical emergency; the outcome depends on how fast the core temperature is lowered, for example by cold water immersion or evaporative cooling. Antipyretics do not work."),
('A woman with anaphylaxis is still hypotensive after two doses of IM adrenaline. What is given next while help comes?',
 'IV fluid bolus', ['Oral antihistamine', 'IV hydrocortisone alone', 'Nebulised salbutamol'],
 'Refractory anaphylaxis needs a rapid IV crystalloid bolus for the leaked plasma, then an adrenaline infusion under expert care. Antihistamines and steroids do not treat shock.'),
("A woman has morning stiffness lasting over an hour and swelling of the small joints of both hands. Which antibody is most specific?",
 "Anti-CCP antibody", ["Rheumatoid factor", "Antinuclear antibody", "ANCA"],
 "Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies are more specific for rheumatoid arthritis than rheumatoid factor, which also occurs in other conditions and healthy older people."),
("A man has a hot, swollen big toe joint. Aspirate shows needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Gout", ["Pseudogout", "Septic arthritis", "Reactive arthritis"],
 "Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped and negatively birefringent. Pseudogout crystals are rhomboid and weakly positively birefringent."),
("A young woman has a facial rash, joint pains and proteinuria. Which antibody is most specific for her likely disease?",
 "Anti-double-stranded DNA", ["Anti-histone antibody", "Anti-centromere antibody", "Anti-Jo-1 antibody"],
 "Anti-dsDNA is highly specific for SLE and tracks lupus nephritis activity. Anti-histone antibodies suggest drug-induced lupus."),
("A whole family has itching, worse at night, with burrows between the fingers. How is it treated?",
 "Permethrin for all contacts", ["Oral steroids for the child", "Antifungal cream only", "Antihistamines only"],
 "Scabies is treated with permethrin 5% applied to the whole body of every household contact at the same time, repeated after a week, with bedding washed."),
("A man with schizophrenia has failed two antipsychotics. Clozapine is started. What must be monitored?",
 "Neutrophil count", ["Serum lithium", "Thyroid antibodies", "Urine ketones"],
 "Clozapine can cause agranulocytosis, so full blood counts are checked weekly at first and then less often."),
("A man who drinks heavily is admitted for surgery and on day two becomes tremulous, sweaty and agitated. Which drug is first-line?",
 "Chlordiazepoxide", ["IM haloperidol", "Oral naltrexone", "Oral disulfiram"],
 "Alcohol withdrawal is treated with a long-acting benzodiazepine such as chlordiazepoxide, plus thiamine. Disulfiram and naltrexone are for relapse prevention later."),
("A woman has fatigue, a low MCV and a serum ferritin of 6 micrograms per litre. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Iron deficiency anaemia", ["Anaemia of chronic disease", "Thalassaemia trait", "Sideroblastic anaemia"],
 "A low ferritin confirms depleted iron stores. In anaemia of chronic disease ferritin is normal or high."),
("A 50-year-old man has a very high white cell count with all stages of myeloid cells and a large spleen. The Philadelphia chromosome is found. Which drug is used?",
 "Imatinib", ["Rituximab", "All-trans retinoic acid", "Hydroxyurea alone"],
 "Chronic myeloid leukaemia is driven by the BCR-ABL fusion from t(9;22). Imatinib, a tyrosine kinase inhibitor, is first-line; ATRA treats promyelocytic leukaemia."),
]
